<a href="#section-configure" id="section-configure" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
<h2>Choose a retry schedule</h2>
<section><div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-java small">MailSendRetryPolicy retry = MailSendRetryPolicy.builder()
    .withMaximumAttempts(3)
    .withRetryDelay(Duration.ofSeconds(30))
    .withRetryBackoff(2, Duration.ofMinutes(5))
    .withRetryJitter(0.2)
    .build();

Mailer mailer = mail.mailerBuilder()
    .withSMTPServer("smtp.example.org", 587, "user", "password")
    .withMailSendRetryPolicy(retry)
    .withSendHandlers(MailSendHandlers.builder().withResultObserver(outcome -> outcome.getDiagnostics().ifPresent(report -> log.info("{}", report))).build())
    .buildMailer();

MailSend&lt;MailSubmissionReceipt&gt; send = mailer.async().sendMail(email);
MailSubmissionReceipt receipt = send.getCompletion().join();</code></pre>
</div><div class="wide">
  <p>Three means at most three provider invocations, including the first. This example waits around thirty seconds after the first refusal,
    around sixty after the second, and stops after a third. The maximum caps the delay after jitter too.
    Attempts and initial delay are required to enable retries; there is no implicit retry schedule.</p>
  <p>Without backoff, delays stay fixed. Without jitter, they do not vary. <code>resetRetryBackoff()</code> and <code>resetRetryJitter()</code>
    remove those optional choices. Calling <code>withMailSendRetryPolicy(...)</code> again replaces the entire policy, including resource limits;
    <code>clearMailSendRetryPolicy()</code> disables retries and restores built-in resource limits unless locked configuration prevents the change.</p>
  <p>On the policy builder, <code>clearRetrySchedule()</code> removes attempts, delay, backoff and jitter while keeping your resource budgets.
    <code>clearMaximumAttempts()</code> and <code>clearRetryDelay()</code> remove only their own choice; supply the missing part again or clear the
    complete schedule before building. Clearing a schedule is not the same as replacing the entire policy.</p>
  <p>Synchronous calls wait on their calling thread. Ordinary asynchronous sends release their worker, lease and proxy before backoff,
    then resume through the same configured executor. Caller-runs executors may execute on the submitting or retry-dispatch thread.</p>
</div></section>

<a href="#section-safety" id="section-safety" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
<h2>Which failures qualify?</h2>
<section><div class="wide">
  <p>The receipt must say <code>REJECTED</code> and <code>SAFE_TO_RETRY_ALL</code>, with structured temporary SMTP evidence such as a 450 recipient refusal
    or a 451 refusal of the complete message. Contradictory permanent, accepted or uncertain facts stop automatic retrying.</p>
  <div class="doc-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="Automatic retry safety"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Automatic action</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Entire unchanged envelope safely refused temporarily</td><td>Wait, then try again within the configured limit</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Permanent refusal</td><td>Fail normally; unchanged content is not a remedy</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Some recipients accepted</td><td>Fail with the partial receipt; never replay the whole email</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Final reply lost or insufficient evidence</td><td>Fail with uncertainty; do not risk a duplicate</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Local validation, configuration, capability or replay-limit failure</td><td>Stop; do not submit again</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Accepted, then cleanup failed</td><td>Fail while retaining acceptance; do not resend</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
  <p>Each physical attempt uses the same frozen MIME bytes, Message-ID and envelope. Templates, attachments and protection are not rebuilt.
    Exact EML and signed content stay unchanged; normal composed submissions still omit headers such as BCC.
    Automatic ENVID is fresh for each physical SMTP transaction; an explicitly fixed ENVID remains fixed.</p>
  <p>Once submission starts, retries never select another relay or redo failover. Failure to reacquire the pinned destination ends the operation.
    Third-party adapters need explicit repeatable-submission support and trustworthy receipt facts. <code>CustomMailer</code> is invoked once
    and owns any internal retrying; logging-only mode makes no SMTP attempts.</p>
  <p>SMTP acceptance is responsibility for onward delivery, not final delivery. Later bounces and server-side delivery retries are separate.</p>
</div></section>

<a href="#section-ownership" id="section-ownership" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
<h2>Which Mailer owns what?</h2>
<section><div class="wide">
  <p>If A selects B through clustering or failover, B supplies the schedule from its retry policy and A supplies its own policy's resource limits.
    A disabled schedule on B does not inherit A's schedule. Grouping these settings in one policy does not change their owners.</p>
  <div class="doc-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="Retry ownership"><table><thead><tr><th>Concern</th><th>Owner</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td>Attempts, delay, backoff and jitter</td><td>Actual selected Mailer</td></tr>
    <tr><td>SMTP settings, templates, security and sending allowance</td><td>Actual selected Mailer; every invocation consumes allowance</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Executor, admission, overall deadline, completion and observer</td><td>Initiating Mailer</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Waiting capacity and replay-memory limits</td><td>Initiating Mailer</td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>
  <p>The observer receives one terminal outcome, not one callback per attempt. The deadline is not reset for another attempt.
    <code>requestCancellation()</code> wakes a waiting retry. Graceful shutdown rejects new work but drains accepted retries before closing owned resources;
    a long configured delay can lengthen shutdown. Borrowed executors must remain usable until the Mailer has drained.</p>
  <p>Simple batches finish retries for the current email before advancing their lazy iterator, and still stop at the first terminal failure.
    Open-connection callbacks execute once. Both intentionally retain their connection and existing thread during retry waits;
    configure the server's idle timeout accordingly. An unusable retained connection is not silently replaced.</p>
</div></section>

<a href="#section-resources" id="section-resources" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
<h2>Bound waiting work and replay memory</h2>
<section><div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-java small">MailSendRetryPolicy retry = MailSendRetryPolicy.builder()
    .withMaximumAttempts(3)
    .withRetryDelay(Duration.ofSeconds(30))
    .withWaitingCapacity(100)
    .withMaximumMessageSize(32L * 1024 * 1024)
    .withMemoryLimit(128L * 1024 * 1024)
    .build();

mailerBuilder.withMailSendRetryPolicy(retry);</code></pre>
</div><div class="wide">
  <p>These are built-in resource defaults, not a default retry rate. Waiting capacity bounds ordinary async sends in backoff, awaiting dispatch
    or queued for resumed execution. It does not replace the initial admission queue, and does not count synchronous or retained-scope waits.</p>
  <p>The per-message limit is serialized MIME size, not attachment size. The aggregate counts retained replay bytes across the initiating Mailer's
    retry-enabled sends, not exact heap use: objects, arrays and provider buffers have overhead. Capture is incremental and uses no temporary files.</p>
  <p>Message and memory-limit failures stop before the first provider invocation. Exhausted waiting capacity or rejected resumption preserves the last
    genuine SMTP refusal, with a suppressed explanation of why no further attempt started. Limits are not silently bypassed.</p>
  <p><code>resetWaitingCapacity()</code>, <code>resetMaximumMessageSize()</code> and <code>resetMemoryLimit()</code> on the policy builder
    restore the built-in values. Configuration locks still apply when that policy is supplied to the Mailer builder.</p>
  <p>You can configure only these limits without enabling retries on this Mailer. For example, A may select a retry-enabled B while
    A retains its own waiting and memory budget. Supply no attempt count, delay, backoff or jitter to keep A's schedule disabled:</p>
</div><div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-java small">MailSendRetryPolicy resources = MailSendRetryPolicy.builder()
    .withWaitingCapacity(20)
    .withMaximumMessageSize(8L * 1024 * 1024)
    .withMemoryLimit(64L * 1024 * 1024)
    .build();

initiatingMailerBuilder.withMailSendRetryPolicy(resources);
// resources.isRetryEnabled() == false</code></pre>
</div><div class="wide">
  <p><code>mailer.getOperationalConfig().getMailSendRetryPolicy()</code> always returns the resolved policy. Use
    <code>isRetryEnabled()</code> to check whether it includes a schedule; resource limits remain available either way.</p>
</div></section>

<a href="#section-results" id="section-results" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
<h2>Read the final result and attempt history</h2>
<section><div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-java small">outcome.getDiagnostics().ifPresent(report -> {
    report.getRetryWait().getElapsed().ifPresent(metrics::recordRetryWaiting);
    for (MailSendSubmissionStep step : report.getSubmissionHistory()) {
        log.info("Attempt {}: {}, code={}, elapsed={}, next delay={}",
            step.getAttemptNumber(), step.getSubmissionStatus(),
            step.getSmtpReplyCode(), step.getElapsed(), step.getRetryDelay());
    }
});</code></pre>
<pre><code class="language-text small">Mail send timings:
  Total: PT30.16S
  Preparation: PT0.002S
  Scheduling: PT0.004S
  Sending-limit wait: not measured: No sending limit is configured for this destination.
  Retry wait: PT30.006S
  Connection acquisition: PT0.026S
  MIME preparation: PT0.004S
  Submission: PT0.1S
  Cleanup: PT0.018S
  Connection history:
    - Connection acquired in PT0.016S
    - Connection acquired in PT0.01S
  Submission history:
    - Attempt 1: REJECTED (SMTP 451), submission=PT0.04S, next delay=PT30S; Temporary refusal; the entire email can be submitted again safely.
    - Attempt 2: ACCEPTED (SMTP 250), submission=PT0.06S</code></pre>
</div><div class="wide">
  <p>This illustrative output has one final successful outcome. Exhaustion fails with the final genuine exception and receipt, not a replacement
    retry-exhaustion exception. Partial failure stays failure. Retry wait includes backoff and waiting for resumed execution; initial scheduling stays separate.</p>
  <p>Acquisition, submission and cleanup accumulate across attempts. Retained scopes exclude shared setup/teardown.
    History records real provider invocations, not failed reacquisitions. It is collected only with an observer and a retry-enabled SMTP send.</p>
  <p>At most 64 entries are retained: the first 63 and the latest. <code>getOmittedSubmissionSteps()</code> counts omitted intermediate entries without
    limiting attempts. Automatic rendering excludes content, addresses, identifiers, endpoints and raw server prose. Read the final receipt for recipient-level facts.</p>
  <p>The first scheduled retry is logged at WARN, later retries at DEBUG, and recovery at INFO.
    A connection probe describes setup and capabilities now, not this email's attempt history; see <a href="/debugging.html">Diagnostics</a>.</p>
</div></section>

<a href="#section-configuration" id="section-configuration" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
<h2>Properties, Spring and CLI</h2>
<section><div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-properties small">simplejavamail.smtp.retry.attempts=3
simplejavamail.smtp.retry.delay=PT30S
simplejavamail.smtp.retry.backoffmultiplier=2
simplejavamail.smtp.retry.maximumdelay=PT5M
simplejavamail.smtp.retry.jitter=0.2

simplejavamail.defaults.mailsend.retry.waitingcapacity=100
simplejavamail.defaults.mailsend.retry.maximummessagesize=33554432
simplejavamail.defaults.mailsend.retry.memorylimit=134217728</code></pre>
</div><div class="wide">
  <p>If you set any scheduling property under <code>simplejavamail.smtp.retry.</code>, supply both <code>attempts</code> and <code>delay</code>.
    Increasing backoff also requires <code>maximumdelay</code>.</p>
  <p>The resource limits under <code>simplejavamail.defaults.mailsend.retry.</code> can be set on their own; they do not enable retries.
    For example, you can limit the initiating Mailer's waiting capacity and replay memory while a selected Mailer supplies the retry schedule.</p>
  <p>Named definitions use the same tails,
    for example <code>simplejavamail.mailers.secondary.smtp.retry.attempts</code> and its delay.
    <a href="/configuration.html#section-locked-configuration">Locks</a> use the existing namespace, such as <code>simplejavamail.locked.smtp.retry.attempts</code>.</p>
  <p>The <a href="/spring.html">Spring Environment integration</a> reads these same typed properties, with ordinary and locked IDE metadata.
    <a href="/debugging.html">Configuration diagnostics</a> show their individual winning sources.</p>
</div><div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-text small">--mailer:withMailSendRetryAttempts 3
--mailer:withMailSendRetryDelay PT30S
--mailer:withMailSendRetryBackoff 2 PT5M
--mailer:withMailSendRetryJitter 0.2
--mailer:withMailSendRetryWaitingCapacity 100
--mailer:withMailSendRetryMaximumMessageSize 33554432
--mailer:withMailSendRetryMemoryLimit 134217728</code></pre>
</div><div class="wide"><p>CLI sends wait for their final result. Configuration and explicit options participate in daemon profile identity,
  so requests with different retry behavior cannot accidentally share a Mailer.</p></div></section>
<section><p class="wide"><code>--mailer:clearMailSendRetryPolicy</code> removes an ordinary property-loaded schedule.
  Locked configuration still prevents clearing. Resource reset options restore their built-in bounds independently of enabling retries.</p></section>

<a href="#section-jobs" id="section-jobs" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
<h2>Keep durable jobs in your application</h2>
<section><div class="wide">
  <p>Built-in retries retain work only in this process. Keep invoices, campaigns, priorities, business expiry and restart recovery in your job system.
    The final observer is the handoff back to that system, not an intermediate failure callback.</p>
  <p>After terminal completion, inspect receipt status and temporary evidence again before scheduling new work. Never restart whole-envelope retrying
    after partial or uncertain acceptance. Recipient-subset retries need an application-owned email and authorization, including template-added recipients.
    The <a href="/smtp-connection-failover.html#section-retry">observer/job-queue recipe</a> covers that broader decision.</p>
</div></section>
